Lepton: VA-API direct output and GPU copy-back conversion (test-25, experimental)

Three ways to deliver hardware-decoded frames, chosen per decoder session:

- Copy-back (default, patches 0023-0029): download and convert on the CPU.
- debug.ffmpeg-codec2.copyback-vpp=true: for BT.709 limited-range P010, the
  GPU converts to NV12 (scale_vaapi) before the download; CPU converts NV12.
- Direct output: debug.ffmpeg-codec2.hwaccel.drm=true with
  debug.ffmpeg-codec2.pixel_format=YUV_420_PLANER (upstream's "VPP mode").
  FFmpeg decodes into its own VA surfaces; the GPU converts each frame into
  the Android YV12 buffer itself, imported as a VA surface. No readback and
  no CPU conversion. Fixes to upstream's VPP mode:
  * no 64-alignment of the advertised picture size; the buffer is aligned,
    the blit covers the picture only and the output is cropped to it;
  * same matrix in and out (as copy-back), from the frame's tags, and both
    sides declared full range so no driver remaps values (Mesa 26.0's VA
    post-processing otherwise squeezes limited range again);
  * wait for the decode before the blit and for the blit before the buffer
    reaches the framework; keep the decoded surface until the blit is done;
  * request the same buffers as copy-back (CPU-mappable YV12): minigbm
    refuses YV12 for decoder-only usage;
  * describe Android's YV12 as I420 with the U plane first: Mesa writes a
    YV12 import's chroma in I420 order;
  * imports of ByteBuffer-mode buffers (no BufferQueue slot) are destroyed
    when their frame is released;
  * any failure, and interlaced frames, fall back to copy-back for the rest
    of the session (LEPTON_VA_DIRECT fallback=...).

Diagnostics: LEPTON_VA_SESSION output=, LEPTON_VA_DIRECT, LEPTON_VA_DIRECT_END,
LEPTON_VA_VPP_END, LEPTON_VA_SLOW, LEPTON_VA_PERCENTILES, LEPTON_VA_DEADLINE.
Test hooks: debug.ffmpeg-codec2.vpp-validation=pixels|timestamps|init-error|frame-error;
with direct output, pixels compares two frames of each session with the
decoded surface (LEPTON_VA_DIRECT_PIXELS).

